WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:08.573
Auth authorizes a payment, then tells capture, which captures it 5 milliseconds later.

2
00:00:08.767 --> 00:00:11.267
Auth's clock runs 40 milliseconds fast.

3
00:00:11.267 --> 00:00:16.268
Sorted by timestamp, the capture comes before the authorization it depends on.

4
00:00:16.467 --> 00:00:23.385
What we need is weaker than real time: if one event could have caused another, the cause must get the smaller stamp.

5
00:00:23.567 --> 00:00:26.885
Leslie Lamport's answer is a counter on each node.

6
00:00:26.885 --> 00:00:29.340
Before each event, the node adds one.

7
00:00:29.633 --> 00:00:32.162
A message carries the sender's counter.

8
00:00:32.162 --> 00:00:35.599
Auth counts the send as 2, and the message carries 2.

9
00:00:35.767 --> 00:00:37.295
Capture's counter is 1.

10
00:00:37.295 --> 00:00:41.413
On receipt it takes the larger of the two, 2, and adds one: 3.

11
00:00:41.413 --> 00:00:44.469
The capture now sorts after the authorization.

12
00:00:44.667 --> 00:00:46.986
Capture sends to the ledger with 4.

13
00:00:46.986 --> 00:00:51.293
The ledger has counted to 5 on its own, so the receive becomes 6.

14
00:00:51.467 --> 00:00:55.548
A counter only grows along a node, and jumps past every message.

15
00:00:55.548 --> 00:00:59.693
So a cause always gets a smaller number, whatever the clocks say.

16
00:00:59.900 --> 00:01:02.465
But a smaller number doesn't mean a cause.

17
00:01:02.465 --> 00:01:07.658
Auth's send has 2 and the ledger's third event 3, yet neither could affect the other.

18
00:01:07.658 --> 00:01:10.651
Lamport stamps can't spot such concurrent events.

19
00:01:10.767 --> 00:01:14.180
A counter also says nothing about the time of day.

20
00:01:14.180 --> 00:01:19.164
A hybrid logical clock keeps both: a wall-clock time and a small counter.

21
00:01:19.367 --> 00:01:22.531
Auth stamps its events with its own clock's time.

22
00:01:22.531 --> 00:01:25.695
Two in the same millisecond get counters 0 and 1.

23
00:01:25.867 --> 00:01:29.539
Capture's clock is 35 milliseconds behind the message.

24
00:01:29.539 --> 00:01:33.347
So capture keeps the message's time and counts on, to 2.

25
00:01:33.567 --> 00:01:37.986
Once its own clock passes that time, the counter starts again at zero.

26
00:01:37.986 --> 00:01:41.900
With clocks a few milliseconds apart, it's almost always zero.

27
00:01:42.100 --> 00:01:48.272
Count along each node and jump past every message, and a cause always sorts before its effect.
